{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794914500531,"sessionID":"ses_e16c29ae3ffeOBXo75aiTjuQ8B","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_46bf8a2fba284f05a2625aec","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"python3 -c \"\nfrom datetime import datetime\nfrom decimal import Decimal\nfrom parcels.delivery import add_working_days\nfrom parcels.packing import parcel_weights\nimport datetime as dt\nprint('2026-11-20 is', dt.date(2026,11,20).strftime('%A'))\n# weekend skipping detail\nprint('add_working_days(Sat 2026-11-21, 1) =', add_working_days(dt.date(2026,11,21),1))\nprint('add_working_days(Fri 2026-11-20, 2) =', add_working_days(dt.date(2026,11,20),2))\n# rounding overshoot\nw = parcel_weights(Decimal('70')); print('70kg ->', w, 'sum', sum(w))\nw = parcel_weights(Decimal('100')); print('100kg ->', w, 'sum', sum(w))\nw = parcel_weights(Decimal('200')); print('200kg ->', w)\n# max per parcel check\nimport math\nprint('ceil kg billed per parcel for 23.334 =', math.ceil(Decimal('23.334')))\n\"\n"},"output":"2026-11-20 is Friday\nadd_working_days(Sat 2026-11-21, 1) = 2026-11-23\nadd_working_days(Fri 2026-11-20, 2) = 2026-11-24\n70kg -> [Decimal('23.334'), Decimal('23.334'), Decimal('23.334')] sum 70.002\n100kg -> [Decimal('25.000'), Decimal('25.000'), Decimal('25.000'), Decimal('25.000')] sum 100.000\n200kg -> [Decimal('28.572'), Decimal('28.572'), Decimal('28.572'), Decimal('28.572'), Decimal('28.572'), Decimal('28.572'), Decimal('28.572')]\nceil kg billed per parcel for 23.334 = 24\n","metadata":{"output":"2026-11-20 is Friday\nadd_working_days(Sat 2026-11-21, 1) = 2026-11-23\nadd_working_days(Fri 2026-11-20, 2) = 2026-11-24\n70kg -> [Decimal('23.334'), Decimal('23.334'), Decimal('23.334')] sum 70.002\n100kg -> [Decimal('25.000'), Decimal('25.000'), Decimal('25.000'), Decimal('25.000')] sum 100.000\n200kg -> [Decimal('28.572'), Decimal('28.572'), Decimal('28.572'), Decimal('28.572'), Decimal('28.572'), Decimal('28.572'), Decimal('28.572')]\nceil kg billed per parcel for 23.334 = 24\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"python3 -c \"\nfrom datetime import datetime\nfrom decimal import Decimal\nfrom parcels.delivery import add_working_days\nfrom parcels.packing import parcel_weights\nimport datetime as dt\nprint('2026-11-20 is', dt.date(2026,11,20).strftime('%A'))\n# weekend skipping detail\nprint('add_working_days(Sat 2026-11-21, 1) =', add_working_days(dt.date(2026,11,21),1))\nprint('add_working_days(Fri 2026-11-20, 2) =', add_working_days(dt.date(2026,11,20),2))\n# rounding overshoot\nw = parcel_weights(Decimal('70')); print('70kg ->', w, 'sum', sum(w))\nw = parcel_weights(Decimal('100')); print('100kg ->', w, 'sum', sum(w))\nw = parcel_weights(Decimal('200')); print('200kg ->', w)\n# max per parcel check\nimport math\nprint('ceil kg billed per parcel for 23.334 =', math.ceil(Decimal('23.334')))\n\"\n","time":{"start":1794914500457,"end":1794914500530}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Note the cost 70kg DE: 66.45. Let's verify: 3 parcels of 23.334 kg → ceil = 24 kg each. postnl: base 4.10 + 0.45*24 = 4.10+10.80 = 14.90; EU surcharge 7.25 → 22.15 each; ×3 = 66.45. ✓\n\nNote the weight per parcel is rounded up to the gram, which may push it slightly over MAX_PARCEL_KG in theory? Let's check: total_kg/count ≤ MAX always, then quantized up to gram — could that exceed MAX? E.g., MAX = 31.5, count = ceil(total/31.5). If total = 31.6, count=2, each=15.8. If total = 63.0, count = 2, each = 31.5. If total = 63.1, count = 3, each=21.034. Since count = ceil(total/MAX), each = total/count ≤ MAX, but rounding up to the gram could exceed MAX if MAX isn't a whole number of grams — MAX=31.5 is whole grams, so total/count ≤ 31.5 and rounding up to 0.001 could give 31.500 exactly at most... Actually if total/count = 31.4999, ceil to 31.500, which is ≤ MAX. So fine.\n\nBut note a subtle bug: the sum of parcel weights may exceed the original total (rounding up each). E.g., 70 kg → 3 × 23.334 = 70.002. Worth mentioning briefly.\n\nNow, key subtlety for Q2: heavy order → split into multiple parcels, and shipping cost is the sum of each parcel's price (quote.py:33). Also note the price per parcel uses math.ceil(weight_kg) started kg.\n\nQ1: free shipping from 60.00 EUR, changeable via env var PARCELS_FREE_FROM (settings.py:15). Note that the settings module reads env once at import time (settings.py:1 docstring). So changing the env var requires restarting the process / re-import.\n\nImportant nuance: the free-shipping check `order_value >= FREE_SHIPPING_FROM` — at or above. And it's after the EU destination check, so it applies only to EU destinations (CannotShip raised before). Also note: order_value must be after discounts.\n\nAlso, careful: `FREE_SHIPPING_FROM = Decimal(os.environ.get(...))` — if the env var is malformed, Decimal raises InvalidOperation at import time.\n\nQ3: default carrier is \"postnl\" (settings.py:10), configurable via PARCELS_CARRIER env var, must be one of dhl/ups/postnl else RuntimeError at import (settings.py:8, 11-12). Value is lowercased.\n\nQ4: Friday at 17:00 → 17 >= CUTOFF_HOUR(15) → start = Saturday. Then add_working_days(Saturday, HANDLING_DAYS + transit) — skips Sat/Sun, so the first working day counted is Monday. HANDLING_DAYS = 1, transit for NL=1 → 2 working days: Mon (1st), Tue (2nd) → Tuesday Nov 24. For DE → transit 2 → 3 working days → Wednesday. Wait, but the question doesn't specify country. Let me explain per country and note that the country matters.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: `start = ordered.date()` then if hour >= 15, start += 1 day → Saturday. add_working_days(Saturday, N): current = Saturday; loop: current += 1 → Sunday (weekday 6, not <5, no decrement), then Monday (weekday 0 → decrement). So Saturday itself is never counted even though it's not a working day... correct since start isn't counted at all in add_working_days (it counts days after start). So effectively the weekend Sat+Sun are skipped and counting begins Monday.\n\nWait — careful: add_working_days(start, days) adds `days` working days *after* start. For start=Saturday: the day after Saturday is Sunday (skipped), Monday counted as day 1, Tuesday as day 2. So NL: 1 handling + 1 transit = 2 working days → Tuesday Nov 24, 2026.\n\nBut if the order had been placed Friday at 14:00 (before cutoff), start = Friday, add_working_days(Friday, 2) → Monday(1), Tuesday(2) → also Tuesday. Interesting.\n\nHolidays: NOT accounted for — docstring at delivery.py:15 says \"holidays are not\" skipped. So a public holiday is treated as a normal working day, so delivery dates may be optimistic. Also note: only weekends are skipped; there's no holiday calendar anywhere in the codebase.\n\nLet me verify the \"at or after 15:00\" semantics: `ordered.hour >= CUTOFF_HOUR` — uses hour only, so 15:00 exactly counts as next day, and 14:59 same day. Also minutes are ignored.\n\nAlso, for the delivery date: the result is the date only, no time.\n\nOne more thing to verify for Q2: is there any maximum total weight limit? No — parcel_weights splits into as many parcels as needed, so a 200 kg order → 7 parcels. There's no upper bound; but each parcel must be ≤ 31.5 kg. Also note that the per-parcel price uses ceil of each parcel's weight.\n\nLet me double-check some numbers with a script for exact citations, and confirm the sum-rounding issue.\n\nAlso, I should double check whether there's anything about holidays in tests or elsewhere. Already grepped — only delivery.py:15.\n\nLet me also check git log for any context, and check that there is no other config mechanism (e.g., a config file).\n\nAlso, verify the delivery calc by running the actual code for Friday 17:00 across countries, which I did: NL Tuesday, DE/BE Wednesday, FR Friday (default 4 transit days: 1+4=5 working days from Sat → Mon,Tue,Wed,Thu,Fri → Friday). Good.\n\nLet me also double check the exact date: Nov 20, 2026 is a Friday? The output shows \"friday17 start-> 2026-11-20\" and delivery dates Nov 24 = Tuesday. Let me verify Nov 20 2026 is a Friday.\n\nAlso for the answer, I want to check the behavior when the order value is exactly 60 and the country is non-EU (CannotShip raised first). Yes — the EU check precedes the free check (quote.py:29-32).\n\nLet me check git log briefly and confirm python version note (repo says 3.11+, local python is 3.10.15 — fine, code works).\n\nLet me run a couple more checks.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1e93dbf2a0012GN18iPAOkPDFB","sessionID":"ses_e16c29ae3ffeOBXo75aiTjuQ8B","messageID":"msg_1e93d98c2001LMRsuh3zXSrueQ"}}
