{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794914509072,"sessionID":"ses_e16c29ae3ffeOBXo75aiTjuQ8B","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_6ede666f49da46fbbaf5565f","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"python3 -m unittest discover -s tests 2>&1 | tail -5"},"output":"...\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\nRan 3 tests in 0.000s\n\nOK\n","metadata":{"output":"...\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\nRan 3 tests in 0.000s\n\nOK\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"python3 -m unittest discover -s tests 2>&1 | tail -5","time":{"start":1794914508986,"end":1794914509071}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me verify the Friday 17:00 delivery calculation details, and also check the holiday question. The `add_working_days` docstring says holidays are not skipped.\n\nLet me trace Friday 2026-11-20 17:00 for NL:\n- ordered.hour = 17 >= CUTOFF_HOUR 15 → start = 2026-11-21 (Saturday)\n- transit for NL = 1, HANDLING_DAYS = 1 → 2 working days from Saturday\n- add_working_days(Sat 21, 2): current = Sat → +1 = Sun (weekday 6, not <5, no decrement) → +1 = Mon 23 (weekday 0, decrement → days=1) → +1 = Tue 24 (decrement → days=0). Result: Tuesday 2026-11-24.\n\nWait — but there's a subtlety: the start date being a Saturday, the weekend skip happens. But note: the cutoff pushes start to Saturday; then Saturday itself is skipped as a non-working day because add_working_days always adds at least 1 day before checking. Actually the loop always increments first, so start day itself is never counted as a working day. So Saturday start → first counted day is Monday.\n\nAlso important: CUTOFF_HOUR check uses `ordered.hour >= CUTOFF_HOUR` — only hour, not minutes. So 17:00 → next day. But note if the order is placed Friday at 17:00 for a country other than the transit table, DEFAULT_TRANSIT_DAYS = 4.\n\nInteresting nuance for Friday 17:00: the start becomes Saturday, then the weekend is skipped, so effectively Monday. But what if Friday at 14:00 (before cutoff)? start = Friday, add_working_days(Fri, 2) = Tuesday as well. Let me check: Fri 20 → +1 Sat (skip) → Sun (skip) → Mon (1) → Tue (2) → Tuesday 24. Same result. Interesting — so the cutoff on Friday 17:00 doesn't actually push past the weekend because of the weekend skipping. Good detail to mention.\n\nLet me verify: add_working_days(Fri 2026-11-20, 2) = 2026-11-24 — yes, Tuesday. Same as delivery_date output.\n\nAlso holidays: no holiday calendar in the codebase at all. The docstring explicitly says holidays are not skipped (parcels/delivery.py:15). So public holidays are treated as normal working days — which can promise a delivery date on a public holiday.\n\nLet me also double check the \"heavier than a single parcel\" question more carefully — the MAX_PARCEL_KG is 31.5. But is there a limit where the order can't be split? No limit on the total; it's split into ceil(total/31.5) parcels of equal weight. Each parcel is priced separately (quote.py:33 sums parcel prices). The parcel weight rounded up to the gram with ROUND_CEILING, which means total can slightly exceed the original (70 → 70.002). Also note that price uses math.ceil(weight_kg) started kilograms — so a 23.334 kg parcel is billed as 24 kg.\n\nAlso note: MAX_PARCEL_KG is not configurable via env (settings.py:17 hardcoded).\n\nOne more nuance for Q1: FREE_SHIPPING_FROM is read from PARCELS_FREE_FROM env var (settings.py:15), default 60.00. Comparison is `order_value >= FREE_SHIPPING_FROM` (quote.py:31), so exactly 60.00 is free. Note the \"inside the EU\" comment; and the CannotShip check comes first, so non-EU raises before the free shipping check (quote.py:29-30). Also the env var is read at import time (settings.py:1 docstring), so changing it requires restarting the process / re-importing.\n\nFor Q3: default carrier is \"postnl\" (settings.py:10), configurable via PARCELS_CARRIER env var; must be one of dhl/ups/postnl else RuntimeError at import (settings.py:11-12). Value is lowercased. Note the check happens at import time. TARIFFS in carriers.py:16-20.\n\nAlso a subtlety: `CARRIER` is validated against CARRIERS at settings import; tariff lookup `TARIFFS[CARRIER]` in quote.py:18.\n\nLet me also check the rounding behavior of the free shipping threshold comparison — Decimal(\"60.00\") vs Decimal(\"60\") → equal, fine.\n\nAlso note for Q1: since env is read at import time, changing PARCELS_FREE_FROM at runtime (e.g. os.environ set after import) has no effect.\n\nLet me check the delivery date for a Friday 17:00 order — the country matters for transit. Let me write up per-country results. Let me compute for all: NL=1, BE=2, DE=2, LU=2, others=4.\n\nFor Friday 2026-11-20 17:00 (start Sat 21):\n- NL: HANDLING 1 + 1 = 2 working days → Mon 23 is 1, Tue 24 is 2 → Tue Nov 24\n- DE/BE/LU: 1+2=3 → Mon, Tue, Wed → Wed Nov 25\n- Others: 1+4=5 → Mon 23, Tue 24, Wed 25, Thu 26, Fri 27 → Fri Nov 27\n\nWait, but need to be careful: the weekend skipping happens because start is Saturday. Let me check: for DE, add_working_days(Sat 21, 3): +1 Sun (skip) +1 Mon (1) +1 Tue (2) +1 Wed (3) → Wed 25. Yes.\n\nInteresting: for a non-Friday Saturday start, e.g. order placed Saturday 10:00 (before cutoff): start = Saturday 21, same result. Hmm, actually add_working_days with start Saturday skips Sat and Sun. But what if an order is placed on a Monday at 14:00 (before cutoff)? start = Monday, days=2 → Tue, Wed. Fine.\n\nBut note a subtlety: for the Friday-17:00 case, the cutoff moves start to Saturday which is a non-working day; add_working_days treats start day as not counted anyway (always increments first). So it's effectively Monday. Same as before-cutoff Friday order. Good.\n\nLet me verify the exact behavior with the actual code once more for a couple of countries and print the intermediate steps.\n\nAlso the holiday question: no holiday calendar exists; the docstring explicitly states holidays are not skipped. So an order placed before a public holiday would be promised a delivery that lands on the holiday (or the transit counting would include holiday days). Let me demonstrate: e.g., if a public holiday is on Monday Nov 23, the code would still count it as a working day → Tuesday Nov 24 delivery, even though the carrier isn't delivering.\n\nLet me verify the tests pass too (just to note nothing broken), and check git diff shows no changes. I must not change any code — only add ANSWERS.md.\n\nLet me double-check line numbers for citations:\n- settings.py:15 FREE_SHIPPING_FROM, settings.py:14 comment\n- quote.py:31-32 free shipping\n- quote.py:29-30 CannotShip\n- packing.py:20-22, packing.py:14-15 docstring, settings.py:17 MAX_PARCEL_KG\n- quote.py:33 sum of parcel prices, quote.py:19 math.ceil per kg\n- settings.py:8-12 carrier\n- carriers.py:16-20 tariffs\n- delivery.py:9-11 transit/cutoff, delivery.py:14-22 add_working_days, delivery.py:25-30 delivery_date, delivery.py:15 docstring re holidays\n- settings.py:18 HANDLING_DAYS\n\nLet me run the test suite quickly and confirm the environment.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1e93de0b3001Q6xkOaT14kwzmg","sessionID":"ses_e16c29ae3ffeOBXo75aiTjuQ8B","messageID":"msg_1e93dc01a001ZG2FMhCK6usNCv"}}
