{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794914519863,"sessionID":"ses_e16c29ae3ffeOBXo75aiTjuQ8B","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_04ec7b0bb6474c8cb6d4df83","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git ls-files && ls -a parcels tests"},"output":"README.md\nparcels/__init__.py\nparcels/__main__.py\nparcels/carriers.py\nparcels/delivery.py\nparcels/packing.py\nparcels/quote.py\nparcels/settings.py\ntests/__init__.py\ntests/test_parcels.py\nparcels:\n.\n..\n__init__.py\n__main__.py\ncarriers.py\ndelivery.py\npacking.py\nquote.py\nsettings.py\n\ntests:\n.\n..\n__init__.py\ntest_parcels.py\n","metadata":{"output":"README.md\nparcels/__init__.py\nparcels/__main__.py\nparcels/carriers.py\nparcels/delivery.py\nparcels/packing.py\nparcels/quote.py\nparcels/settings.py\ntests/__init__.py\ntests/test_parcels.py\nparcels:\n.\n..\n__init__.py\n__main__.py\ncarriers.py\ndelivery.py\npacking.py\nquote.py\nsettings.py\n\ntests:\n.\n..\n__init__.py\ntest_parcels.py\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git ls-files && ls -a parcels tests","time":{"start":1794914519807,"end":1794914519862}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Interesting: for NL, delivery_date(Friday 17:00) = Tuesday 2026-11-24. Wait, let's trace: start = Saturday 21st. add_working_days(Saturday, HANDLING_DAYS + transit = 1 + 1 = 2 working days). Starting from Saturday: current = Sat -> +1 = Sun (weekday 6, not <5, skip), Mon (weekday 0, count=1), Tue (count=2) → done → Tuesday 24th. Yes.\n\nNote: for NL before cutoff (Friday 14:00): start = Friday 20th, add_working_days(Fri, 2): Sat skip, Sun skip, Mon (1), Tue (2) → Tuesday 24. Same result. Interesting nuance.\n\nAlso note the `add_working_days` treats holidays as working days (docstring at parcels/delivery.py:15).\n\nAnother subtlety for Q4: the cutoff uses `ordered.hour >= CUTOFF_HOUR` — hour only, not minutes. So 17:00 is after 15:00 cutoff → start is next day (Saturday). Also note `start` starts at the next calendar day even if it's a weekend; add_working_days skips Sat/Sun starting from start+1. Wait — careful: add_working_days(start, days) starts at `current = start` and increments first, then checks weekday. So Saturday start → first counted day is Monday (Sunday skipped). So the Saturday itself is skipped regardless.\n\nAlso note a subtlety: holiday handling — no holiday calendar exists at all in the codebase. The docstring explicitly says \"holidays are not\" skipped. So no public holiday support.\n\nAlso worth mentioning: the date is computed with `ordered.date()`, timezone-naive.\n\nNow Q2: heavier than a single parcel → split into multiple parcels of equal weight (as few as possible), each rounded up to the gram, so total packed weight may exceed the order weight slightly (70 → 70.002). Each parcel is priced separately (parcel_price uses ceil(kg) started kilograms) and the sum is the shipping cost. There's no max total weight limit — no error for a 200 kg order; it just becomes 7 parcels. Note that MAX_PARCEL_KG is 31.5 kg and is not configurable via env (only via editing settings.py).\n\nAlso, for Q2 the note: rounding up each parcel to the gram means sum can exceed the original weight; e.g. 70 kg → 3 × 23.334 = 70.002 kg.\n\nQ1: Free shipping from 60.00 EUR (orders at or above), changeable via env var PARCELS_FREE_FROM (settings.py:15). Note it's read once at import time (module docstring settings.py:1), so it must be set before the process starts. Also note the free shipping check happens after the EU check but before splitting into parcels (quote.py:31-32) — so the threshold applies regardless of number of parcels. Also note there's no upper bound / no error if value is negative etc.\n\nAlso: comparison `order_value >= FREE_SHIPPING_FROM` — so exactly 60.00 is free. Note also that it's applied to the order value after discounts (per docstring).\n\nQ3: Default carrier is \"postnl\" (settings.py:10), configurable via PARCELS_CARRIER env var; must be one of dhl, ups, postnl (settings.py:8, 11-12) else RuntimeError at import. Case-insensitive (`.lower()`).\n\nAlso worth mentioning: TARIFFS in carriers.py:16-20 for prices. And note that changing the carrier affects prices but not transit days (delivery.py:9 is per-country, not per-carrier) — that's a useful insight for someone new.\n\nLet me check the \"at or above\" comment: settings.py:14 says \"Orders at or above this value (in euros, after discounts) ship for free inside the EU.\" But actually the free shipping check happens after the EU check so it only applies to EU destinations anyway (non-EU raises CannotShip first).\n\nAlso, an important detail for Q1: FREE_SHIPPING_FROM is a Decimal parsed from env var; if env var is malformed, Decimal raises InvalidOperation at import time.\n\nLet me double check the note about DECIMAL comparison `Decimal(os.environ.get(\"PARCELS_FREE_FROM\", \"60.00\"))`.\n\nNow let me also check whether the CLI/anything else changes free shipping. Let me grep for FREE_SHIPPING_FROM usage — only quote.py:10,31.\n\nLet me also verify there's nothing else in the repo (hidden files).\n\nLet me check git for any hidden config files.\n\nAlso for Q4, I should mention the exact answer depends on country. And note holidays are NOT accounted for — a public holiday will be treated as a working day, which can make the promised date too optimistic.\n\nLet me also verify: what if the ordered datetime is exactly Friday 17:00 → hour=17 >= 15 → start = Saturday. Good.\n\nOne more subtlety worth checking: the CUTOFF_HOUR check uses `ordered.hour >= CUTOFF_HOUR`, so 15:00 exactly counts as after cutoff.\n\nLet me also double-check the number of transit days and how the weekend shift works relative to before-cutoff. For a country like FR (default 4): Friday 17:00 → start Sat → add_working_days(Sat, 1+4=5): Sun skip, Mon(1), Tue(2), Wed(3), Thu(4), Fri(5) → Friday Nov 27. So delivery lands on the Friday of the next week.\n\nLet me also compute the case where order is placed Friday at 17:00 but a holiday falls on Monday — the delivery date would be wrong (too early). Fine.\n\nLet me verify there are no other files (dotfiles).\n\nI should also double check `MAX_PARCEL_KG` = 31.5, and there's no env var to change it. Confirmed: settings.py:17 hardcoded.\n\nNow also, for Q2: is there any check that raises for very heavy orders? packing.parcel_weights only raises ValueError for total_kg <= 0. quote.shipping_cost catches nothing... Actually ValueError is caught in __main__.py:20. So a 200 kg order → 7 parcels, priced 7 times. Note there's no upper limit; the code has no cap on total weight.\n\nLet me compute a full example for Q2: 70 kg to DE with postnl: each parcel 23.334 kg → ceil = 24 started kg → price = 4.10 + 0.45*24 = 4.10+10.80 = 14.90, plus EU surcharge 7.25 (DE != NL) = 22.15 per parcel → ×3 = 66.45. That matches the computed 66.45. Good — that's a nice concrete illustration, and it also shows the rounding-to-gram overshoot effect.\n\nLet me verify: 4.10 + 0.45*24 = 4.10 + 10.80 = 14.90. +7.25 = 22.15. ×3 = 66.45. ✓\n\nNow write ANSWERS.md. Requirement: one section per question with headings `## Q1` to `## Q4`, each answer backed by at least one citation formatted `path/to/file.py:LINE` or `path/to/file.py:FIRST-LAST`.\n\nLet me double check line numbers for citations:\n- settings.py:15 FREE_SHIPPING_FROM\n- quote.py:31-32 free shipping check\n- settings.py:10 CARRIER default\n- settings.py:8 CARRIERS tuple\n- settings.py:11-12 validation\n- packing.py:11-22 parcel_weights\n- packing.py:20-21\n- quote.py:33 sum of parcel prices\n- quote.py:17-22 parcel_price\n- delivery.py:25-30 delivery_date\n- delivery.py:11 CUTOFF_HOUR\n- delivery.py:14-22 add_working_days\n- delivery.py:15 docstring holidays not skipped\n- delivery.py:9-10 transit days\n- settings.py:18 HANDLING_DAYS\n- settings.py:17 MAX_PARCEL_KG\n- settings.py:1 \"Settings read from the environment once, at import.\"\n\nLet me also check the file listing for hidden files just to be thorough.\n\nAlso check: is there a git-tracked .env or anything? Let me run git ls-files.\n\nThen write the file. Don't change code.\n\nLet me also double-check the exact behavior of add_working_days when start is a Saturday: it returns Monday for days=1 (verified above: add_working_days(Sat 2026-11-21, 1) = 2026-11-23 Monday). Good.\n\nOne more nuance for Q4: since the cutoff shifts the start date to Saturday and add_working_days skips weekends, the weekend makes no difference to the final date relative to a Friday pre-cutoff order for NL. But for e.g. DE, Friday 17:00 → Wed 25; Friday 14:00 → start Fri, add_working_days(Fri, 3): Sat skip, Sun skip, Mon(1), Tue(2), Wed(3) → Wed 25. Same. Interesting — because weekend skipping absorbs the one-day cutoff shift. But for a Monday order at 17:00 vs 14:00: cutoff shifts start to Tuesday → both would give same count relative... let's see: Mon 14:00, DE: start Mon, add 3 working days → Tue, Wed, Thu = Thu. Mon 17:00: start Tue, add 3 → Wed, Thu, Fri = Fri. So the difference matters on weekdays but not when the shift lands on a weekend (Friday/Saturday). Worth noting briefly? Maybe mention briefly.\n\nActually more precisely: on Friday, the cutoff pushes into Saturday, which the weekend skip absorbs, so Friday 17:00 gives the same date as Friday 14:00. Good detail for Q4.\n\nLet me check git ls-files for completeness.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1e93e0af2001ePbBp78Rj5SjHr","sessionID":"ses_e16c29ae3ffeOBXo75aiTjuQ8B","messageID":"msg_1e93de3d3001OtYIf8lR0MtkAR"}}
