arcels.settings import FREE_SHIPPING_FROM\nfrom parcels.quote import shipping_cost\nprint('threshold', FREE_SHIPPING_FROM)\nprint('80 EUR order:', shipping_cost(Decimal('80'), Decimal('2'), 'NL'))\n\"\necho '---carrier---'\nPARCELS_CARRIER=DHL python3 -c \"\nfrom parcels.settings import CARRIER\nfrom parcels.quote import shipping_cost\nfrom decimal import Decimal\nprint('carrier', CARRIER)\nprint('2kg NL:', shipping_cost(Decimal('10'), Decimal('2'), 'NL'))\n\"\necho '---bad carrier---'\nPARCELS_CARRIER=upsx python3 -c \"import parcels.settings\" 2>&1 | tail -2\necho '---bad threshold---'\nPARCELS_FREE_FROM=abc python3 -c \"import parcels.settings\" 2>&1 | tail -2","time":{"start":1794914527921,"end":1794914528053}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now let me verify a few edge details:\n\nQ1: Free shipping from 60.00 EUR (orders at or above). Changeable via env var `PARCELS_FREE_FROM`. Note it's read at import time (settings.py:15, docstring line 1 \"Settings read from the environment once, at import.\"). Note also the free-shipping threshold is compared to order_value after discounts — quote.py:31 `if order_value >= FREE_SHIPPING_FROM`. Also note: the free shipping check happens after the EU check, so non-EU raises CannotShip regardless. Also note: free shipping applies inside the EU (comment says \"inside the EU\"). And the free-shipping check happens BEFORE parcel splitting, so weight doesn't matter for free shipping.\n\nAlso note: env var is read at import time so changing it requires restarting the process / re-importing.\n\nQ2: Heavy order is split into multiple parcels, as few as possible, equal weight each, at most MAX_PARCEL_KG (31.5 kg) each. Note the rounding: each parcel weight is rounded UP to the gram with ROUND_CEILING, so the total packed weight can slightly exceed the original order weight (70 kg → 3 × 23.334 = 70.002 kg). Each parcel is priced separately (quote.py:33 sums parcel prices), and each parcel's price uses `math.ceil(weight_kg)` started kg (quote.py:19) — so 23.334 kg is billed as 24 kg. No error is raised for heavy orders; there's no upper limit on total order weight. The `MAX_PARCEL_KG` is not configurable via env var — it's a hardcoded Decimal at settings.py:17.\n\nAlso note: `count = max(1, ...)` so zero/negative weight raises ValueError at packing.py:19 (\"an order weighs something\").\n\nQ3: Default carrier = \"postnl\" (settings.py:10), chosen via env var `PARCELS_CARRIER`; valid values are dhl, ups, postnl (settings.py:8), validated at import time with RuntimeError if invalid (settings.py:11-12). Value is lowercased. Tariffs in carriers.py:16-20.\n\nImportant caveat: settings are read at import time, so changing the env var requires a new process (or reloading the module). Also `CARRIER` is imported into quote.py at module level (quote.py:10), so it's fixed at import.\n\nQ4: Order Friday at 17:00, country dependent.\n- CUTOFF_HOUR = 15 (delivery.py:11), so at 17:00 >= 15 → start = Saturday (delivery.py:27-28).\n- Then add_working_days(start, HANDLING_DAYS + transit) where HANDLING_DAYS=1 (settings.py:18) and transit is per-country (delivery.py:9-10): NL=1, BE/DE/LU=2, everything else 4.\n- add_working_days skips Sat/Sun (delivery.py:14-22).\n\nWait — careful. The start is Saturday 2026-11-21. add_working_days starts with `current = start` (Saturday), then adds 1 day → Sunday, weekday()=6, not <5, so doesn't count. Then Monday (weekday 0) counts. So the days count begins Monday.\n\nFor NL: days = 1 + 1 = 2 working days → Monday counts as 1, Tuesday counts as 2 → 2026-11-24 Tuesday. ✓ matches output.\n\nInteresting nuance: an order placed Friday at 14:00 (before cutoff) also gives Tuesday for NL: start = Friday, add_working_days(Fri, 2): Sat skip, Sun skip, Mon=1, Tue=2 → Tuesday 2026-11-24. So the cutoff has no effect on Friday because the weekend absorbs the extra day. That's a nice insight — for NL, Friday before and after cutoff give the same delivery date. Let me double check: delivery_date(datetime(2026,11,20,14,0),'NL') → 2026-11-24. Yes same.\n\nFor DE/BE/LU: days = 1 + 2 = 3 → Mon, Tue, Wed → 2026-11-25 Wednesday.\nFor others (default 4): days = 5 → Mon,Tue,Wed,Thu,Fri → 2026-11-27 Friday.\n\nHolidays: NOT accounted for. The docstring explicitly says \"holidays are not\" skipped (delivery.py:15). So a public holiday is treated as a normal working day — the estimated delivery date could land on a public holiday, which is a real gap worth flagging.\n\nAlso note transit days are per-country and independent of the carrier — the same regardless of DHL/UPS/PostNL (delivery.py:9).\n\nLet me double-check the cutoff: `ordered.hour >= CUTOFF_HOUR` — only hour, not minutes. So 15:00 exactly counts as after cutoff. Also it uses local/naive datetime — `datetime.now()` in __main__.py:24, no timezone handling.\n\nAnother subtlety for Q4: `add_working_days` counts the day strictly after start, so if start itself is a working day it isn't counted. And if start is Saturday, then Monday is the first working day. Fine.\n\nOne more subtlety worth verifying for Q2: the claim about the \"started kilogram\" — math.ceil(weight_kg) at quote.py:19. 23.334 → 24 started kg, so each parcel is billed at base + 24*per_kg. Confirmed.\n\nAlso for Q2: is there any upper bound/rejection for very heavy orders? No. Let's check: total_kg = 200 → 7 parcels. There's no max total order weight. The only failure is weight <= 0.\n\nLet me also verify what happens with the rounding overshoot in pricing: 70 kg DE = 66.45 as computed.\n\nNow, is there anything about how the free shipping threshold interacts with parcel splitting? Free shipping short-circuits before parcel_weights is called (quote.py:31-33), so no splitting needed.\n\nOne more check for Q1: \"how can that value be changed without editing code?\" → set env var PARCELS_FREE_FROM. But note: it's read at import time (settings.py docstring line 1), so it must be set before the process starts/imports parcels.settings. Also note there's no validation that it's a valid Decimal — a bad value raises InvalidOperation at import. Let me verify that.\n\nAlso note FREE_SHIPPING_FROM applies to order_value \"after discounts\" (settings.py:14 comment, quote.py:26 docstring) — the caller must pass the discounted value.\n\nLet me test env var behavior quickly.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1e93e29fc001Ec3tlPTyPPltTn","sessionID":"ses_e16c29ae3ffeOBXo75aiTjuQ8B","messageID":"msg_1e93e0bc0001QPeQvANk955OuC"}}
