{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794914537387,"sessionID":"ses_e16c29ae3ffeOBXo75aiTjuQ8B","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_77e6f48180cf495bad989671","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"python3 -c \"\nfrom datetime import datetime\nfrom parcels.delivery import delivery_date\nfor o in [datetime(2026,11,22,10,0), datetime(2026,11,22,16,0), datetime(2026,11,20,15,0), datetime(2026,11,20,14,59)]:\n    print(o.strftime('%A %H:%M'), '->', delivery_date(o,'NL'), delivery_date(o,'NL').strftime('%A'))\n\""},"output":"Sunday 10:00 -> 2026-11-24 Tuesday\nSunday 16:00 -> 2026-11-25 Wednesday\nFriday 15:00 -> 2026-11-24 Tuesday\nFriday 14:59 -> 2026-11-24 Tuesday\n","metadata":{"output":"Sunday 10:00 -> 2026-11-24 Tuesday\nSunday 16:00 -> 2026-11-25 Wednesday\nFriday 15:00 -> 2026-11-24 Tuesday\nFriday 14:59 -> 2026-11-24 Tuesday\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"python3 -c \"\nfrom datetime import datetime\nfrom parcels.delivery import delivery_date\nfor o in [datetime(2026,11,22,10,0), datetime(2026,11,22,16,0), datetime(2026,11,20,15,0), datetime(2026,11,20,14,59)]:\n    print(o.strftime('%A %H:%M'), '->', delivery_date(o,'NL'), delivery_date(o,'NL').strftime('%A'))\n\"","time":{"start":1794914537351,"end":1794914537386}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now let me verify the details for the Friday 17:00 case precisely.\n\nOrder Friday at 17:00:\n- CUTOFF_HOUR = 15; ordered.hour = 17 >= 15 → start = Friday + 1 day = Saturday.\n- Then add_working_days(Saturday, HANDLING_DAYS + transit).\n- add_working_days starts at current = start (Saturday), then advances day by day, counting only weekdays.\n\nFor NL: transit = 1, HANDLING_DAYS = 1, so days = 2. Starting from Saturday 2026-11-21: +1 → Sunday (weekend, skipped), +1 → Monday 23 (count 1), +1 → Tuesday 24 (count 2). Result: Tuesday 2026-11-24.\n\nWait — but there's a subtlety. `start` is Saturday, and the while loop starts by adding a day first. So Saturday itself is never counted (it's not a weekday anyway). So for NL: delivery Tue 24 Nov. Interesting: an order placed Friday at 14:00 (before cutoff) would start Friday, then +1 Sat (skip), +1 Sun (skip), +1 Mon (1), +1 Tue (2) → also Tuesday 24. So both same for NL. But for DE: 3 days. Friday 17:00 → start Sat → Mon(1) Tue(2) Wed(3) = Wed 25. Friday 14:00 → start Fri → Mon(1) Tue(2) Wed(3) = Wed 25. Also same. Hmm, in general for orders on Friday after cutoff vs before, the results coincide because the weekend absorbs the extra day. Let me double check: cutoff pushes start by one calendar day (Fri→Sat). add_working_days from Sat: first working day after Sat is Mon. From Fri: first working day after Fri is Mon. So yes, identical.\n\nBut careful — what about the \"default\" transit 4 (e.g., FR): 5 working days. Friday 17:00 → Sat start → Mon(1) Tue(2) Wed(3) Thu(4) Fri(5) → Friday 27 Nov. Friday 14:00 → Fri start → Mon(1) ... Fri(5) → Friday 27. Same.\n\nNote an edge: Friday at exactly 15:00 → cutoff applies (>=15).\n\nNow holidays: the docstring at delivery.py:15 explicitly says \"holidays are not\" skipped. There is no holiday calendar in the codebase. So weekends are handled, public holidays are NOT accounted for — a holiday that falls on a weekday will still be counted as a working day, so the quoted date may be too optimistic (delivery on a holiday / carrier not delivering).\n\nAlso worth noting: a subtle behavior — the cutoff hour is a hardcoded constant at delivery.py:11, not configurable via env. Only PARCELS_CARRIER and PARCELS_FREE_FROM are configurable. Also HANDLING_DAYS, MAX_PARCEL_KG are hardcoded in settings.py:17-18.\n\nAlso Q2: heavier than one parcel → split into multiple parcels of equal weight, each ≤ MAX_PARCEL_KG (31.5 kg). Note: rounding up to the gram with ROUND_CEILING means total packed weight can slightly exceed the actual weight (70 kg → 3 × 23.334 = 70.002 kg). Also each parcel is billed separately at base + per_kg × ceil(weight) — so 23.334 kg bills as 24 started kg, which is the rounding-to-started-kg rule at quote.py:19.\n\nNote: there's no upper bound on total weight — the code just splits into more parcels. There's no error for very heavy orders. Also note the MAX_PARCEL_KG=31.5 is presumably the PostNL limit but is hardcoded and not env-configurable.\n\nLet me also double check the edge case for count: total_kg = 31.6 → count = ceil(31.6/31.5) = 2 → each = 15.8. Fine.\n\nEdge: total_kg = 63.0 → count = ceil(63/31.5) = 2 → each = 31.5 → fine. total = 63.1 → count = 3 → each = 21.034.\n\nQ3: default carrier is postnl (settings.py:10), chosen by env var PARCELS_CARRIER, valid values dhl/ups/postnl (settings.py:8, 11-12), case-insensitive via .lower(). Tariff table carriers.py:16-20. Invalid value raises RuntimeError at import time. Note settings are read at import, so changing the env var requires a new process (or reimport).\n\nAlso note: the transit time in delivery.py:9 is NOT carrier-specific — the same TRANSIT_DAYS applies regardless of carrier. That's a worthwhile caveat for Q3/Q4.\n\nQ1: Free shipping from 60.00 EUR (inclusive, `>=`), for EU destinations only — non-EU raises CannotShip before the free check (quote.py:29-32). Changed via PARCELS_FREE_FROM env var (settings.py:15). Note: it's read once at import (settings.py:1 docstring). Note the free shipping threshold check happens before packing, so a heavy order over threshold is free regardless of parcels.\n\nAlso note the threshold compares order_value after discounts (per docstring at quote.py:26).\n\nLet me also check: shipping_cost with order_value >= threshold returns Decimal(\"0.00\") even for multi-parcel.\n\nOne more check on Q1: is the free-shipping threshold applied for non-EU? quote.py:29-30 raises CannotShip first, so no.\n\nAlso Q4 detail: delivery_date uses ordered.hour >= CUTOFF_HOUR — note it uses the hour only, so 15:00:00 onward is \"next day\". There's no timezone handling; datetime is naive (naive local time). The `__main__` uses datetime.now() (local naive).\n\nAlso note HANDLING_DAYS = 1 is added to transit (delivery.py:30).\n\nLet me compute the exact answer table for a Friday 17:00 order:\n- NL (transit 1): Tue 24 Nov\n- DE/BE/LU (transit 2): Wed 25 Nov\n- All other EU (transit 4): Fri 27 Nov\n\nWait — need to double check the add_working_days start when start is Saturday and days=1: current=Sat, days=1 → current += 1 → Sun, weekday 6, not counted → current += 1 → Mon, counted, days=0 → returns Mon. Yes.\n\nInteresting edge: If an order is placed Saturday at 10:00 (before cutoff), start = Saturday, then same as above → Mon for NL... no wait, days=2 → Mon(1), Tue(2) → Tue. So an order placed Saturday morning NL delivers Tuesday. And Sunday → same. But note: weekend start is handled naturally since add_working_days skips weekends.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtle bug worth flagging: cutoff at 15:00 pushes to \"next day\" — but the next day might be Saturday, and add_working_days handles it. But consider order placed Sunday 16:00 → start = Monday (day after Sunday) → then add_working_days from Monday counts Tue, Wed... So Sunday 16:00 → start Monday → transit 2 → Wed. Whereas Sunday 10:00 → start Sunday → Mon, Tue counted → Tue. So the cutoff creates a one-working-day jump on weekends. Interesting: Sunday 16:00 delivers Wednesday (NL: Tue vs Wed). Let's verify: Sunday 10:00 NL: start=Sunday, days=2 → Mon(1) Tue(2) → Tue. Sunday 16:00: hour 16 >= 15 → start = Monday → days=2 → Tue(1) Wed(2) → Wed. Yes! That's a real quirk: orders after the cutoff on Sunday get delivered a day later than orders before the cutoff on Sunday. Worth mentioning briefly as a caveat.\n\nLet me verify with the code.\n\nAlso, another caveat: the cutoff uses `ordered.hour >= CUTOFF_HOUR` regardless of minutes — 15:00 exactly counts as after cutoff.\n\nLet me verify the Sunday thing and also test the holiday behavior (no holiday support → a holiday on a weekday is counted as a working day).\n\nLet me also check git history for any hints — only one commit \"fixture\".\n\nLet me run the checks.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1e93e4f7e001dmxs3yxtZ5F9x2","sessionID":"ses_e16c29ae3ffeOBXo75aiTjuQ8B","messageID":"msg_1e93e2b9a00102jBakJNfK8tOj"}}
