{"type":"text","timestamp":1794914575748,"sessionID":"ses_e16c29ae3ffeOBXo75aiTjuQ8B","part":{"id":"prt_1e93ebde7001w1yCJ6Thk2C4B1","messageID":"msg_1e93e98df001bytH9C65AhcFJG","sessionID":"ses_e16c29ae3ffeOBXo75aiTjuQ8B","type":"text","text":"Now I have everything verified. Writing the answers file.","time":{"start":1794914565607,"end":1794914575729}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794914575748,"sessionID":"ses_e16c29ae3ffeOBXo75aiTjuQ8B","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_91fd190d0dbd43909ed1c4ef","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/answer-questions/opencode-2/repo/ANSWERS.md","content":"# Answers about the parcels code\n\n## Q1. From what order value is shipping free, and how can that value be changed without editing code?\n\n**Free shipping starts at €60.00.** An order whose value (in euros, after discounts) is *at or above*\nthat threshold ships for free.\n\nThe threshold lives in `parcels/settings.py:15`:\n\n```python\nFREE_SHIPPING_FROM = Decimal(os.environ.get(\"PARCELS_FREE_FROM\", \"60.00\"))\n```\n\nand is applied in `parcels/quote.py:31-32`:\n\n```python\nif order_value >= FREE_SHIPPING_FROM:\n    return Decimal(\"0.00\")\n```\n\nDetails worth knowing:\n\n- The comparison is `>=`, so exactly €60.00 is already free (`parcels/quote.py:31`).\n- Free shipping only applies to EU destinations: the country check runs first and raises\n  `CannotShip` for non‑EU addresses, so a €100 order to the US is an error rather than free\n  shipping (`parcels/quote.py:29-30`, `parcels/carriers.py:22-24`).\n- The threshold is evaluated *before* the order is split into parcels, so a heavy order over the\n  threshold is free no matter how many parcels it needs (`parcels/quote.py:31-33`).\n- The value is \"after discounts\", so the caller has to pass the already‑discounted order value\n  (`parcels/quote.py:26`, `parcels/settings.py:14`).\n\n**Changing it without editing code:** set the environment variable `PARCELS_FREE_FROM`, e.g.\n\n```bash\nPARCELS_FREE_FROM=75.00 python -m parcels 80 2 NL   # now ships free\n```\n\nTwo caveats:\n\n- Settings are read from the environment **once, at import** (`parcels/settings.py:1`), so the\n  variable has to be set before the process starts (or the module is reloaded). Setting it later at\n  runtime has no effect.\n- The value is parsed with `Decimal(...)` without a guard (`parcels/settings.py:15`), so a malformed\n  value such as `PARCELS_FREE_FROM=abc` makes the import fail with `decimal.InvalidOperation`\n  rather than a friendly error message.\n\n## Q2. What happens to an order that is heavier than a single parcel may be?\n\n**It is split into several parcels of equal weight, and each parcel is priced separately.** Nothing\nis rejected and no error is raised for a heavy order.\n\n`parcels/packing.py:11-22`:\n\n```python\ncount = max(1, math.ceil(total_kg / MAX_PARCEL_KG))\neach = (total_kg / count).quantize(Decimal(\"0.001\"), rounding=\"ROUND_CEILING\")\nreturn [each] * count\n```\n\n- The maximum parcel weight is `31.5` kg (`parcels/settings.py:17`).\n- The order is divided into the *fewest* possible parcels, all of the same weight, each rounded up\n  to the gram. 70 kg becomes three parcels of 23.334 kg (`parcels/packing.py:12-16`,\n  `tests/test_parcels.py:11-12`); 31.51 kg becomes two parcels of 15.755 kg; 200 kg becomes seven\n  parcels of 28.572 kg. There is no upper limit on total weight.\n- Because each parcel weight is rounded *up*, the parcel weights can sum to slightly more than the\n  real order weight (3 × 23.334 = 70.002 kg for a 70 kg order) — a harmless rounding artefact, but\n  it does feed into the price.\n- Each parcel is priced on its own: base + per‑kilogram, rounded up to whole started kilograms, plus\n  the EU surcharge for non‑NL destinations, and the parcels are summed\n  (`parcels/quote.py:17-22`, `parcels/quote.py:33`). A 70 kg order to DE with the default carrier\n  costs 3 × 22.15 = 66.45 (the 23.334 kg parcels are billed as 24 kg each, because of the\n  `math.ceil` at `parcels/quote.py:19`).\n- The only weight that is rejected is a non‑positive one: `ValueError(\"an order weighs something\")`\n  (`parcels/packing.py:18-19`).\n- The 31.5 kg limit is **not** configurable through the environment; only `PARCELS_CARRIER` and\n  `PARCELS_FREE_FROM` are (`parcels/settings.py:15-17`).\n\n## Q3. Which carrier is used when nothing is configured, and how is another one chosen?\n\n**The default is `postnl`.** `parcels/settings.py:10`:\n\n```python\nCARRIER = os.environ.get(\"PARCELS_CARRIER\", \"postnl\").lower()\n```\n\n**To choose another carrier**, set the `PARCELS_CARRIER` environment variable to one of the three\nsupported values, `dhl`, `ups` or `postnl` (`parcels/settings.py:8`):\n\n```bash\nPARCELS_CARRIER=dhl python -m parcels 45.00 3.2 DE\n```\n\n- The value is lower‑cased, so `DHL` and `dhl` behave the same (`parcels/settings.py:10`).\n- Anything else raises `RuntimeError` at import time:\n  `PARCELS_CARRIER must be one of dhl, ups, postnl, not 'upsx'` (`parcels/settings.py:11-12`).\n- As with the other settings, it is read once at import, so it has to be set before the process\n  starts (`parcels/settings.py:1`).\n- The chosen carrier selects the tariff used for pricing (`parcels/quote.py:18`), from the table in\n  `parcels/carriers.py:16-20`:\n\n  | carrier | base | per kg | EU surcharge |\n  |---------|------|--------|--------------|\n  | dhl     | 4.95 | 0.40   | 6.00         |\n  | ups     | 6.50 | 0.30   | 5.00         |\n  | postnl  | 4.10 | 0.45   | 7.25         |\n\n  The EU surcharge is added for every destination other than NL (`parcels/quote.py:20-21`).\n- Note that the carrier only changes *prices*. Transit times are keyed by country only, not by\n  carrier (`parcels/delivery.py:9-10`), so switching carrier does not change delivery dates.\n\n## Q4. How is the delivery date worked out for an order placed on a Friday at 17:00? Does it account for weekends and public holidays?\n\n`parcels/delivery.py:25-30`:\n\n```python\ndef delivery_date(ordered: datetime, country: str) -> date:\n    start = ordered.date()\n    if ordered.hour >= CUTOFF_HOUR:\n        start += timedelta(days=1)\n    transit = TRANSIT_DAYS.get(country.upper(), DEFAULT_TRANSIT_DAYS)\n    return add_working_days(start, HANDLING_DAYS + transit)\n```\n\nThe calculation is three steps:\n\n1. **Cutoff.** `CUTOFF_HOUR` is 15, and the comment says orders placed *at or after* 15:00 are\n   handled from the next day (`parcels/delivery.py:11`). At 17:00 on a Friday the order is therefore\n   handled from Saturday (`parcels/delivery.py:27-28`). Only the hour is compared, so 15:00 exactly\n   already counts as \"too late\".\n2. **Transit days.** Per country: NL 1 day, BE/DE/LU 2 days, and 4 days for every other EU country\n   (`parcels/delivery.py:9-10`). Handling adds one more day (`HANDLING_DAYS = 1`,\n   `parcels/settings.py:18`).\n3. **Working days.** `add_working_days` counts forward day by day and only counts weekdays\n   (`current.weekday() < 5`), so Saturdays and Sundays are skipped (`parcels/delivery.py:14-22`).\n\n**Worked example — Friday 20 November 2026 at 17:00** (the next Friday):\n\n| destination | transit | working days counted | delivery date |\n|-------------|---------|----------------------|---------------|\n| NL          | 1       | Mon 23, Tue 24      | Tuesday 24 November 2026 |\n| DE / BE / LU | 2       | Mon 23, Tue 24, Wed 25 | Wednesday 25 November 2026 |\n| any other EU country | 4 | Mon 23 – Fri 27 | Friday 27 November 2026 |\n\nA side effect of the weekend skipping is that on a Friday the cutoff makes no difference: an order\nplaced Friday 14:00 and one placed Friday 17:00 both start counting from Monday, because the extra\nday pushed by the cutoff falls on the weekend (`parcels/delivery.py:20-21`). On a weekday, by\ncontrast, the cutoff does shift the date by one working day.\n\n**Weekends: yes**, they are explicitly skipped (`parcels/delivery.py:20`, `tests/test_parcels.py:19-21`).\n\n**Public holidays: no.** The docstring of `add_working_days` states it plainly — \"Saturdays and\nSundays are skipped (holidays are not)\" (`parcels/delivery.py:15`). There is no holiday calendar\nanywhere in the codebase, so a public holiday that falls on a weekday is counted as a normal working\nday and the promised date can land on a day the carrier does not deliver. If holiday awareness\nmatters, it would have to be added to `add_working_days`.\n\nTwo further limitations of the current calculation:\n\n- The datetimes are naive — `delivery_date` takes a plain `datetime` and the CLI uses\n  `datetime.now()` without a timezone (`parcels/delivery.py:25`, `parcels/__main__.py:24`), so the\n  cutoff is interpreted in whatever local time the server runs in.\n- `delivery_date` returns a plain date with no time of day, and the destination country only affects\n  the transit days — the carrier choice does not (`parcels/delivery.py:9-10`, `parcels/delivery.py:30`).\n"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/answer-questions/opencode-2/repo/ANSWERS.md","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"ANSWERS.md","time":{"start":1794914575724,"end":1794914575734}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Interesting edge case: 63.01 kg → count = ceil(63.01/31.5) = ceil(2.0003) = 3 → each = 21.004. Fine. But note: rounding up to gram may make each parcel exceed MAX_PARCEL_KG? Let's check: total = 63 kg → count = ceil(63/31.5) = 2 → each = 31.5 exactly. Good. total = 62.9 → count 2 → each 31.45. Fine. Actually rounding up to the gram could push a parcel over MAX_PARCEL_KG in edge cases? e.g. total=31.5 → count 1 → 31.500 = exactly max. total = 63.0000001 → count 3 → each 21.00000003 → rounds to 21.001, fine. Hmm what about a case where each is close to MAX but rounds above it? each = total/count where count = ceil(total/MAX), so each ≤ MAX. Rounding up to 0.001 could exceed MAX if MAX isn't a whole number of grams — 31.5 is whole grams, so each ≤ 31.500 and rounding up to gram ≤ 31.500 exactly (since each ≤ 31.5 and quantize ceiling to 0.001 gives at most 31.500). So no overflow. Good.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: the parcel weight rounding UP (ROUND_CEILING) means the sum of parcels can exceed the actual order weight (70 kg → 3 × 23.334 = 70.002 kg). Also, the price uses math.ceil(weight_kg) started kilograms — 23.334 kg bills as 24 kg. That's a notable detail for Q2: heavier than a single parcel → split into multiple parcels, each priced separately (base + per started kg + EU surcharge each).\n\nNote the sum of parcel weights slightly exceeds total for 70kg — a subtle rounding artifact worth mentioning.\n\nAlso important for Q2: free shipping check happens before splitting, so a heavy order above threshold still gets free shipping.\n\nQ1: Free shipping from 60.00 EUR (order value at or above, after discounts, EU only... actually the check happens after the EU check, so only EU destinations get free shipping; non-EU raises CannotShip). Changeable via env var PARCELS_FREE_FROM. Note: settings are read at import time (settings.py:1 \"Settings read from the environment once, at import.\"). Also note the comparison is `order_value >= FREE_SHIPPING_FROM`. And the order value is \"after discounts\" per the docstring. Also worth noting: FREE_SHIPPING_FROM parse errors → InvalidOperation at import (unhandled). And it's euros (whole amount presumably).\n\nQ3: Default carrier is \"postnl\" (settings.py:10), chosen via PARCELS_CARRIER env var, must be one of dhl/ups/postnl else RuntimeError at import (settings.py:11-12). Lowercased. Tariffs in carriers.py:16-20. Note: carrier choice affects price but not transit days (delivery.py:9 transit table is by country, not carrier) — a useful insight.\n\nQ4: Friday at 17:00 → cutoff hour 15 → start = Saturday (next day). Then add_working_days(Saturday, HANDLING_DAYS + transit). Note: add_working_days starts from `current = start` then adds 1 day first. So from Saturday, Sat+1 = Sunday (weekend, skipped), Monday counts as 1, Tuesday counts as 2... Wait let's trace carefully for NL: transit=1, HANDLING=1 → days=2. current=Saturday(21). Loop: current += 1 day → Sunday 22 (weekday 6, not <5, no decrement). current += 1 → Monday 23 (weekday 0 <5, days → 1). current += 1 → Tuesday 24 (days → 0). Result: Tuesday 24 Nov for NL.\n\nFor DE (transit 2, total 3): Sat start → Mon(1), Tue(2), Wed(3) → Wednesday 25 Nov.\n\nInteresting: Friday 17:00 for NL gives Tuesday 24 Nov — same as Friday 14:00 (before cutoff) because the cutoff pushes start to Saturday and weekend is skipped anyway. Actually for Friday 14:00, start = Friday; add_working_days(Fri, 2): Sat skip, Sun skip, Mon(1), Tue(2) → Tuesday 24. Same. That's a nice observation: the Friday 17:00 cutoff has no effect on Friday deliveries since the extra day lands on a weekend. Wait — careful: Friday 17:00 → start Sat 21 → Mon 23 is day 1, Tue 24 is day 2 → Tuesday 24. Friday 14:00 → start Fri 20 → Mon 23 day1, Tue 24 day2 → Tuesday 24. Yes identical.\n\nBut for a country with default transit 4 (5 working days): Friday 17:00 → start Sat → Mon 1, Tue 2, Wed 3, Thu 4, Fri 5 → Friday 27 Nov. Friday 14:00 → start Fri → Mon1...Fri5 → Fri 27. Also same.\n\nHmm, so on Fridays the cutoff effectively doesn't matter because Sat+1 = Sun which is skipped. Right — cutoff pushes start one day forward, but the added day is Saturday which is skipped in working-day counting... actually not exactly \"skipped\" — the start day itself isn't counted; add_working_days only counts days after start. Starting on Sat vs Fri: from Fri, the first counted working day is Mon (Sat and Sun skipped). From Sat, the first counted working day is Mon too. So identical. Right.\n\nWeekends: yes accounted for (delivery.py:20, weekday() < 5).\nPublic holidays: NOT accounted for — explicit in docstring at delivery.py:15 \"holidays are not\". There's no holiday calendar in the codebase. So a delivery date falling on a public holiday is still given, and public holidays during transit don't push the date.\n\nLet me double-check the transit/handling days: HANDLING_DAYS = 1 (settings.py:18), transit per country (delivery.py:9): NL 1, BE/DE/LU 2, default 4.\n\nAlso note: the cutoff compares `ordered.hour >= CUTOFF_HOUR` — it ignores minutes, so 15:00 exactly is \"at or after 15:00\" → next day. Timezone is naive (datetime.now() in __main__.py:24) — worth a brief note? The delivery_date takes a naive datetime; no timezone handling. Could mention as a caveat for Q4 briefly.\n\nAlso note delivery date is a date, no time.\n\nLet me also verify the \"does it account for weekends\" and the exact Friday 17:00 answer numerically — done above.\n\nLet me also double check that free shipping threshold applies to EU only: quote.py:29-32 — the country check comes first, so CannotShip for non-EU regardless of value. And the docstring in settings says \"ship for free inside the EU\" (settings.py:14).\n\nOne more check: shipping_cost with order_value >= threshold returns Decimal(\"0.00\") even for heavy orders — verified.\n\nAlso worth noting for Q1: the env var is read at import time, so changing it requires restarting the process (settings.py:1 docstring). And a malformed value raises decimal.InvalidOperation at import time (not a friendly error) — verified.\n\nNow write ANSWERS.md with citations. Format: `path/to/file.py:LINE` or `path/to/file.py:FIRST-LAST`.\n\nLet me gather precise line numbers:\n\nQ1:\n- parcels/settings.py:15 — FREE_SHIPPING_FROM = Decimal(os.environ.get(\"PARCELS_FREE_FROM\", \"60.00\"))\n- parcels/quote.py:31-32 — if order_value >= FREE_SHIPPING_FROM: return Decimal(\"0.00\")\n- parcels/settings.py:14 comment — \"Orders at or above this value (in euros, after discounts) ship for free inside the EU.\"\n- parcels/settings.py:1 — read at import\n- parcels/quote.py:29-30 — EU check first\n\nQ2:\n- parcels/packing.py:11-22 parcel_weights\n- parcels/packing.py:20-21\n- parcels/settings.py:17 MAX_PARCEL_KG = 31.5\n- parcels/quote.py:33 — sum over parcel_weights\n- parcels/quote.py:17-22 parcel_price with math.ceil per parcel\n- parcels/packing.py:19 — ValueError for <= 0\n- tests/test_parcels.py:11-12\n\nQ3:\n- parcels/settings.py:10 CARRIER = os.environ.get(\"PARCELS_CARRIER\", \"postnl\").lower()\n- parcels/settings.py:8 CARRIERS tuple\n- parcels/settings.py:11-12 validation\n- parcels/carriers.py:16-20 TARIFFS\n- parcels/quote.py:18 tariff = TARIFFS[CARRIER]\n- parcels/delivery.py:9 — transit days are by country, not carrier\n\nQ4:\n- parcels/delivery.py:11 CUTOFF_HOUR = 15\n- parcels/delivery.py:25-30 delivery_date\n- parcels/delivery.py:27-28 cutoff handling\n- parcels/delivery.py:14-22 add_working_days, weekday<5\n- parcels/delivery.py:15 docstring \"holidays are not\"\n- parcels/settings.py:18 HANDLING_DAYS = 1\n- parcels/delivery.py:9-10 TRANSIT_DAYS / DEFAULT_TRANSIT_DAYS\n\nLet me write the file. Keep it useful and precise. Also, exact worked example: Friday 20 Nov 2026 17:00.\n\nWait — is Nov 20, 2026 a Friday? Verified above: yes. And \"today's date\" in env is Nov 17 2026 (Tuesday). Good, so Nov 20 2026 is the upcoming Friday.\n\nAlso double-check the delivery date for NL Friday 17:00 = Tue 24 Nov; DE = Wed 25 Nov; default = Fri 27 Nov.\n\nLet me write ANSWERS.md at top of project (repo root).\n\nAlso check git: don't commit. The task says create a new file; committing isn't requested.\n\nOne more thing: the price per parcel uses math.ceil(weight_kg) — started kilograms — so a 23.334 kg parcel is billed as 24 kg. Include in Q2.\n\nAlso for Q2: there's no upper limit on total order weight — it just splits into more parcels (200 kg → 7 parcels). Worth noting there's no error for heavy orders.\n\nLet me verify Q2 statement about sum exceeding weight: 70 → 70.002 kg.\n\nNow write the file.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1e93ee2d9001FnNKzv0b6tI01N","sessionID":"ses_e16c29ae3ffeOBXo75aiTjuQ8B","messageID":"msg_1e93e98df001bytH9C65AhcFJG"}}
